\section{Common setting}

We work in the accepted faithful natural representation of $G=G_2(11)$
on a seven-dimensional row space $V$ over $\F_{11}$. Its nondegenerate
symmetric bilinear form is denoted by $B$. The identification with the
original group, the faithful change of frame, and membership of the
specified marks in $G$ are inherited premises. Every mark acts as an
isometry of this same form.

The ordered quartets are $(a,b,c,e)$ in the original prefix job $155$.
They have distinct nonidentity involutions and all distant commutations.
The endpoints $a,e$ and the third fixed prefix mark $b$ are common to the
eight rows; $c$ is reconstructed from the original catalogue and its
recorded orbit representative. Original orbit positions, rather than a
new ordering of representatives, identify the applications below.

Put $H=\gen{a,b,c,e}$ and
\[
 p=\tfrac14(I+a)(I+e)\in\F_{11}[H].
\]
For the accepted nonzero seed $v$ and $\delta=B(v,v)\ne0$, the complete
matrix identity checked in each application is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:projection}
 xp=\frac{B(x,v)}{\delta}v\qquad(x\in V).
\end{equation}
In particular, the projector is the actual product of the endpoint
matrices. Checking a cyclic vector by itself would not establish the
argument that follows.

\section{The common linear argument}

The following is the earlier argument on the full matrix algebra,
recalled to specify the certificate's mathematical content.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:algebra}
Assume~\eqref{eq:projection}. If seven vectors $v_i=vh_i$, with
$h_i\in H$, form a basis of $V$, then
$\F_{11}[H]=\operatorname{End}_{\F_{11}}(V)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For every $i,j$, the operator $h_i^{-1}ph_j$ belongs to $\F_{11}[H]$.
Since $h_i$ is an isometry, its action on a row $x$ is
\[
 xh_i^{-1}ph_j
 =\frac{B(xh_i^{-1},v)}{\delta}\,vh_j
 =\frac{B(x,v_i)}{\delta}\,v_j.
\]
Nondegeneracy of $B$ and the basis property show that the seven
functionals $B(-,v_i)/\delta$ form a basis of $V^*$. Their products
with the basis vectors $v_j$ form a basis of $\operatorname{End}(V)$.
All forty-nine of these operators lie in $\F_{11}[H]$, proving the
equality.
\end{proof}

Consequently $H$ acts irreducibly on $V$. Indeed, a nonzero invariant
subspace would be stable under every endomorphism of $V$, which forces
it to be $V$.

The classification premise is Kleidman's complete maximal subgroup
theorem for odd $q$~\cite{K}. In the natural $G_2(11)$ module, every
proper subgroup containing an element of order $37$ lies in a maximal
subgroup of type $\operatorname{SU}_3(11){:}2$. Each such maximal subgroup
preserves a nonisotropic rational line. This geometric description is
recorded in Wilson's Section~4.2.6~\cite{W}. The earlier common note on
generation by prime witnesses records the full maximal subgroup list,
the field restrictions and the precise source editions used here.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:generation}
Assume the preceding model, form and classification premises. Suppose
\eqref{eq:projection} holds and a supplied orbit basis satisfies the
hypotheses of Lemma~\ref{lem:algebra}. If a word $W$ in the four actual
marks satisfies
\[
 Y=W^6\ne I,\qquad Y^{37}=I,
\]
then $H=G$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The nonidentity element $Y$ has order $37$, because $37$ is prime.
If $H<G$, the borrowed maximal subgroup theorem places $H$ in a
conjugate of the unitary normalizer just described. Its invariant line
would be $H$-invariant, contradicting Lemma~\ref{lem:algebra}. Thus $H=G$.
\end{proof}

